SPRING IS NOT ETERNAL
A hardworking peasant was plowing the land under the scorching sun, preparing to plant carrots and onions. Spring was not eternal — he needed to finish the planting as quickly as possible.
Suddenly, a messenger approached him with a scroll. Unrolling it, the messenger read:
"Daimyo Suzuki Takeshi commands the samurai to eliminate the enemy and criminal Nakanu Nishimura from Izumo."
The peasant sighed and answered:
"I accept this task and swear that I will fulfill the will of the Daimyo."
Dropping his hoe, he headed toward the storage room where his armor was kept. On that day, he did not think about money or glory — the samurai's mind was focused only on unfinished work, because spring was not eternal.
In Kurashiki, everything was peaceful. Cherry blossoms were blooming, and playful winds carried their petals through the streets, blowing unusually strong that day.
Looking around, he watched the people: they talked, went about their business, and bargained at the wealthy markets of the city.
"How easy life is for ordinary people. They don't even realize how terrible our world is — a world full of killers and criminals who have no code of honor," the samurai thought, feeling disappointment inside.
Then he saw his shuri and smiled.
"There it is. The sanctuary of honor and justice. My small world of hope for the bright future of Japan."
A massive castle towered on the mountain — a magnificent, multi-layered fortress built from clay and enormous stones. Watchtowers stood along the walls, and at the entrance lay a wide moat with a gigantic drawbridge thirty tsubo long.
As he climbed the stairs, the wind struck his face, and he continued at the same steady pace, lowering his head.
When the samurai reached the castle, he fell to his knees and bowed until his forehead touched the ground.
Then he approached a small tesuwa standing near the entrance to perform purification. It was a stone structure with a small spring flowing inside.
The samurai knelt down and began the ritual: he took a wooden ladle in his right hand, filled it with water, and poured it over his left hand. Then he repeated the process, switching hands.
Now he was ready to enter the sacred shuri.
He no longer thought about his garden or the meaningless lives of ordinary people. His only goal was to fulfill the Daimyo's command — because spring was not eternal.
The samurai called out to the bannin standing guard, and the man lowered the bridge, opening the heart of a warrior's life.
Inside the shuri, nobody sat idle. Some practiced their spirituality, masters trained newcomers, and leaders discussed future military campaigns.
But our samurai headed toward the storage room to take his blade and armor.
At that moment, he thought:
"How sad that I spend so little time in this perfect world of shuri... and how sad that I must leave it now."
After preparing himself, he left the fortress and headed north, toward Izumo, searching for the criminal Nakanu Nishimura.
The first day of the march began. The samurai did not eat, did not drink, did not rest, did not sleep. His body, weighed down by armor, was pulled toward the ground, and his legs barely dragged through rocky mountains — now climbing steep slopes, now descending into valleys. Fatigue pressed on him, but he understood: "The Daimyo will not wait while I loaf around here. I must reach Izumo by tomorrow."
However, soon a river blocked his path. Raging waters roared, rushing in swift currents, and despite their crystal clarity, the river looked impassable. The samurai sat down on the bank, scooped up cold water with his palms, and drank it greedily, feeling the icy freshness restore his strength a little. He thought, staring at the water: "The river always flows from small springs to the great ocean. Why then do I, despite years of service, still live alone, without family or wealth? But be that as it may — I need to cross it."
Sighing, he rose and walked along the shore in search of a bridge. By evening, walking along the river, the samurai saw a village spread out by the water. The settlement looked quiet and modest: instead of roads — narrow worn paths, few livestock, but everywhere there was the smell of fresh fish. The village was famous for its catch, and almost every passerby offered to sell him fish, as if in respect.
Moving toward the pier, the samurai suddenly noticed a young girl running out of one of the huts. She stopped in front of him, deftly adjusting the hem of her simple but clean dress. Her hair was neatly braided, and her face was full of respect. The girl bowed, almost touching her forehead to the ground, and spoke in a soft voice:
"Great warrior, accept our kindness. Stay in our home for the night. In our humble village, we have not seen such honorable guests for many years."
To which the samurai snorted and turned away:
"The Daimyo will not wait while I loaf around and rest in some backwater hovel of poor blind people who see nothing beyond their own noses," he said coldly and sharply, not hiding his contempt.
The smile vanished from her face, and tears rolled down, washing away the rice powder. The girl lowered her head without a word and returned to the house. The samurai, without a drop of regret, headed toward the pier.
At the pier sat fishermen, loudly discussing everyday matters. "How can these people think about such simple and earthly things? All they care about is filling their bellies and sleeping. They have no honor, no awareness of the sinfulness and darkness in our world," the samurai thought to himself.
Suddenly, one of the fishermen looked up from his fishing and addressed the guest:
"Hey, what are you standing there for? Looking for help, warrior? Ask if you need. We'll treat you to fish, point you in the right direction, and even take you across to the other shore."
The samurai replied:
"I need to get to the other side. I have orders from the Daimyo."
The fisherman shrugged and pulled a boat into the water. The boat was slightly rotten and covered in moss — clearly rarely used. Still, despite that, it was sturdy enough to stay afloat. The samurai in heavy armor climbed into the boat, and it sank slightly under his weight. The fisherman was surprised:
"Whoa! How do you walk around in that shell? If I had to carry that kind of weight over long distances, I'd quit. I'm not some ox."
The samurai didn't even get angry at the commoner for such remarks. On the contrary — he thought: isn't he himself some kind of beast? But he didn't have time to dwell on it — the boat hit the shore. The samurai stepped out without a word of thanks and moved on.
The road lay through thick fog that enveloped almost the entire forest. But there was no choice — duty called. Pushing through branches and bushes, the samurai didn't notice a small cliff. His foot slipped on the wet ground, and he tumbled down, landing in the Takahashi River.
Not wanting to die, he struggled to his feet. Fortunately, the river turned out to be shallow, and the current weak, so the samurai managed to keep his balance. Coughing and breathing heavily, he pulled himself onto the opposite bank. "Now I'm soaked... But at least I'm alive. Damn fog."
Even now, with his clothes completely drenched, the samurai did not stop. He kept moving forward, not slowing down. He had to cross mountains, swim rivers, and push through dense forests. And so, after a long journey, he found himself at the edge of Japan, in Izumo Province.
Exhausted, two days without sleep and rest, the samurai finally reached his goal. Stepping out onto the rice paddies, he spotted Nakanu Nishimura himself. He didn't look like a fearsome or ferocious criminal — he seemed like an ordinary worker, harvesting rice.
"Do I really have to kill him? What could this man have done? Maybe he stole rice or tried to ruin the paddies? But is this really my task?"
The samurai's eyes darted, doubts racing through his mind. The thoughts pressed down, tearing him apart from within. What to do now? His instincts screamed — eliminate the source of unease.
Unable to bear the tension, he screamed and, running up to Nakanu, furiously chopped off his head.
"What's wrong with me? Why didn't I want to do this?!" — the samurai whispered through tears. His fingers tingled; he had never felt anything like this before. He looked at his bloody katana with disgust, as if it were foreign to him.
But shaking the blood from the blade, he pulled himself together. "Maybe it's just the lack of sleep," he thought, trying to calm himself, and suppressing the unease, began preparing for the return.
Rain began to fall, and the exhausted samurai, no longer able to endure, noticed a lone house and decided to stop for the night. Approaching the door, he knocked, and soon an elderly woman opened it:
"Oh, brave warrior, come in, come in," she said warmly. "I'm always happy to have guests. You must be hungry and tired. Stay with me until tomorrow."
The samurai could not utter a word and, nodding, knelt down. The woman closed the door and helped him remove his armor. He sat by the irori to warm himself. Soon the hostess handed him miso soup, trying to feed her weary guest.
In the silence, she began to talk about her life:
"I don't live alone, but my son is delayed at work today. How lucky I am that you dropped by — at least some company."
The samurai tensed and began to breathe nervously, suspecting something.
"And what does your son do?" — he asked with anxiety in his voice.
The woman chuckled:
"Oh, don't get me wrong. He's no daimyo or clerk. Just a regular rice collector. He's my only one, we live on pennies. Recently he brought me ten grains of rice, but he was brutally beaten for it. I feel so sorry for him... He was forced to steal just to feed us somehow."
Everything inside the samurai turned upside down. He realized he had killed this woman's son. Clenching his fists and lowering his head, through tears he said:
"Your son is no more..."
The old woman was shocked:
"What do you mean 'no more'? That can't be!"
But the samurai repeated: "Your son is no more. I... killed him."
The woman's face twisted, her lips trembled, tears streaming down like rain. She grabbed a rag and, screaming, began beating the samurai:
"You monster! You killed an innocent man! And how much are they paying you for it? Get out of my house!"
The samurai, struggling to put on his heavy armor, left the house, tormented by thoughts.
"I am an executioner working for food... I saw the world differently than it really is. I saw evil in people, but the real problem is me."
He cast aside all his doubts and promised himself that, no matter what, he would run to the Daimyo's palace.
By evening, he was back in his native Kurashiki.
Exhausted and out of breath, the samurai stumbled and burst into the palace, where a feast was in full swing. The Daimyo's residence was adorned with tatami on the floor, silk curtains, and vases gleaming with gold. The samurai found the dining hall, threw open the doors, and collapsed to the floor, his strength drained.
The Daimyo immediately grew furious:
"How dare you burst into my palace without permission, especially during a visit from such important guests!"
Summoning all his remaining strength, the samurai replied with anger in his voice:
"You are a pig! You made me march two days without rest and kill an innocent man for a pathetic ten grains of rice! And the worst part is — I dared to look his mother in the eyes after this 'successful' deed. That look will haunt me until the end of my days, reminding me of that murder."
The Daimyo only laughed, as if not understanding the gravity of his words:
"Losses are losses, you understand. Don't you want a reward for such an easy task?"
The Daimyo's laughter only fueled the samurai's inner rage. He realized that the system he had served had long rotted away. On the last of his strength, he shouted:
"Then I will deprive you of all the samurai who serve you!"
The Daimyo, intrigued, ordered a hundred samurai standing behind him to step before the traitor.
"Come on, show me how you, barely on your feet, can defeat a hundred brave warriors."
The samurai lowered his head and, with slowly trembling hands, drew his katana and thrust it sharply into his chest. The others followed their comrade's example and all fell as one, soulless bodies collapsing to the ground.
The guests sat in horror, realizing they had witnessed a lake of blood created by the power of the samurai.
The samurai died, never having managed to plant his garden — because spring is not eternal, and neither is the life of a samurai. His dreams of fertile land and bountiful harvest remained unfulfilled, forever hidden in memory, carried away by the wind of time. The spring wind whispered that life, like nature, is full of change, and now in that place — only emptiness and the bitterness of loss.

But he left with his eyes open, as if awaiting new horizons, where his soul could find peace and a new beginning, free from the weight of the past.
